{"editorial_reason":"An open metric ball uses the metric d on its source space X and a strictly positive radius r. The independently screened metric-space definition supplies the distance function; this link does not assert the pseudometric extension not yet individually screened.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.open-metric","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.01","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AOpen_Ball","source_phrase":"metric space","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMetric_Space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.analysis98.metric-space"}
{"editorial_reason":"The open norm-ball condition uses norm displacement ∥v−a∥ on the specified normed vector space, not a metric on a separate arbitrary space. Positive radius is explicit and no completeness requirement is introduced.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.open-norm","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.02","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AOpen_Ball","source_phrase":"normed vector space","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ANormed_Vector_Space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.analysis98.normed-vector-space"}
{"editorial_reason":"A closed metric ball uses non-strict distance inequality d(x,a)≤r over an independently screened metric space. This is not defined to be the topological closure of the corresponding open ball in arbitrary metric spaces.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.closed-metric","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.03","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AClosed_Ball","source_phrase":"metric space","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMetric_Space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.analysis98.metric-space"}
{"editorial_reason":"A closed norm ball takes points satisfying ∥v−a∥≤r within the same normed vector space; completeness is not part of the definition. Its norm-space foundation is separately screened and attributed.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.closed-norm","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.04","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AClosed_Ball","source_phrase":"normed vector space","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ANormed_Vector_Space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.analysis98.normed-vector-space"}
{"editorial_reason":"The homeomorphism is defined between two topological spaces; both domain and codomain have specified open-set systems. The definition does not require either space to be Hausdorff or a metric space.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.homeomorphism-topological.1","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.05","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AHomeomorphism/Topological_Spaces","source_phrase":"topological spaces","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space"}
{"editorial_reason":"The continuous-bijection-with-continuous-inverse characterization directly uses continuity of maps between topological spaces for f and f inverse. It does not claim that a continuous bijection alone is a homeomorphism.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.homeomorphism-topological.1","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.06","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AHomeomorphism/Topological_Spaces","source_phrase":"continuous","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AContinuous_Mapping_%28Topology%29","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation76.continuous-topological-map"}
{"editorial_reason":"The equivalent open-set transport characterization directly quantifies over open subsets in topologies on X and Y. The independently reviewed topological-space meaning gives those open-set axioms.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.homeomorphism-topological.2","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.07","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AHomeomorphism/Topological_Spaces","source_phrase":"topological spaces","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space"}
{"editorial_reason":"This equivalent homeomorphism formulation explicitly presupposes a bijection f:X→Y, which first is a total single-valued map. The mapping target supplies that foundation; bijectivity is an additional stated requirement.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.homeomorphism-topological.2","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.08","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AHomeomorphism/Topological_Spaces","source_phrase":"bijection","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMapping","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.mapping-element-assignment"}
{"editorial_reason":"The directional difference quotient is formed from a real-valued total map f on Euclidean vector inputs and requires values f(a+tu) along a straight line. This source-backed mapping prerequisite does not assert total differentiability follows from gradient existence.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.directional-differentiable","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.09","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ADirectional_Derivative","source_phrase":"f:ℝⁿ→ℝ","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMapping","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.mapping-element-assignment"}
{"editorial_reason":"The directional derivative is a finite two-sided real limit as t tends to zero, with real-valued function values and real Euclidean scalars. The independently reviewed ordered real number system supplies the underlying scalar setting; total differentiability remains an explicit extra assumption.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.directional113.directional-differentiable","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.directional114.10","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_directional_topology_prerequisite_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ADirectional_Derivative","source_phrase":"ℝ","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AReal_Number","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.real45.complete-ordered-real-field"}
